# A first dive into NeoGeo development

Why yet another started project (without having finished any of the recent others)?
Well the 'enthusi' handle has a reason - I'm easily enthusiastic about things.
But I think I'm rather capable of picking up oldish projects and interweaving stuff.
The NeoGeo has caught my attention - before that AES+ announcement, actually.
I always wanted to look into the m68K assembly and even got an Atari ST for that reason but then started on the notorious Alice Matra with its Motorola 6803 CPU - so some early relative if you so want. It's a fun little CPU and I must say the m68k really makes a great first impression when coding in bare assembly! There are C compilers available for the NeoGeo and even a whole DEV kit but bleh, I never liked those and the Playdate is the only device I ever used a devkit for.

Half the fun (for me) is getting into a system by heart and byte.

The NeoGeo is mostly based on sprites but it does have a tile map layer (despite what some sources say) and a nice one, too. 40x32 tiles of which probably 38x28 are safe to use accross various TV formats. And it stores a massive 4,096 of those 8x8 tiles. Each with one out of 16 palettes of 15 colors (+ global transparency). That's massive.
I wrote a converter for that f**ked up tile format and rendered a nature scene <3.
Happy with the result. This very first test running on emulation also showcases some test sprites after the pseudo-bitmap-image.
Enjoy (if you can):
